\section{Point and one-sided hypotheses}{

The form of the equation decides how \code{post_prob} and \code{evid_ratio} are
obtained, and the two routes have different requirements.

A one-sided hypothesis (\code{"dextrt > 0"}) is a tally of posterior draws:
\code{post_prob} is the fraction of draws satisfying the inequality, and nothing
beyond the posterior is needed.

A point hypothesis (\code{"dextrt = 0"}) is a Bayes factor computed by the
Savage-Dickey density ratio, the posterior density at 0 divided by the prior
density at 0.

That Bayes factor is the Bayesian counterpart of the frequentist
likelihood-ratio test, which is what edgeR's \code{glmLRT()} reports for the same
coefficient: both weigh the model with \code{dextrt} free against the same model
with \code{dextrt} held at 0, so both need the null to be a restriction of the
alternative. What differs is the treatment of the parameters. The
likelihood-ratio test maximises the likelihood under each hypothesis and
refers the ratio to a chi-square distribution to get a p-value; the Bayes
factor averages the likelihood over the prior instead, so it needs no null
distribution, but it inherits a dependence on that prior. \code{evid_ratio} is
therefore the analogue of the test statistic and \code{post_prob} its normalised
form, on a scale where 1 is no evidence either way. Neither is a p-value:
there is no error rate being controlled behind them, so do not threshold
them as if there were.

Three things follow from how that ratio is computed, and each of them turns
\code{post_prob} and \code{evid_ratio} into \code{NA} or into noise when it is not met.
\itemize{
\item \strong{It needs prior draws.} The fit has to come from \code{\link[=estimate_gene]{estimate_gene()}} with
\code{sample_prior = "yes"}, which is the default. A fit made with
\code{sample_prior = "no"} has no prior draws and reports \code{NA}.
\item \strong{It needs a proper prior on every parameter tested.} brms cannot draw
from a flat prior, so a parameter left with brms' default flat prior
reports \code{NA} however many draws the fit has. The default prior set built
by \code{\link[=estimate_gene]{estimate_gene()}} is proper throughout; a \code{prior} you supply yourself
replaces it entirely, so keep it proper.
\item \strong{It rests on a density estimate, so it needs draws.} The estimate and
the interval settle long before a density at a single point does. A fit of
a few hundred draws gives a usable \code{ci_lower}/\code{ci_upper} next to a
\code{post_prob} that moves from run to run; around a thousand post-warmup
draws is where it starts to hold still.
}

The ratio also depends on the prior scale rather than on the data alone.
Widening \code{coefficient_prior_scale} in \code{\link[=estimate_gene]{estimate_gene()}} thins prior mass at
0 and so moves the evidence towards the null however clear the data are
(Lindley's paradox), which is why that default is one doubling of
expression rather than something diffuse.

Group-level coefficients are the one case no refit fixes: brms cannot sample
their priors, so a point test with \code{class = "r"} always reports \code{NA}. Since
\code{hypothesis = "random_vs_rest"} builds \verb{= 0} contrasts of exactly those
parameters, read \code{estimate} and the interval there rather than \code{post_prob}.
}

